※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `@[relro]` on a variable at the top level: the constructors write it, and
※ from the startup function on it is read-only.
※
※ **Written once, before the program starts.**  What a program works out once
※ and then only reads -- a table filled in from what the system says, a seed,
※ where something was found -- is a variable that is written by a constructor
※ and read by everything else.  Saying so is what lets the image keep it where
※ nothing can change it after: the variables are in a section of their own,
※ `.pl4g.relro`, which the entry point makes read-only once the constructors
※ have run and before the startup function does.  The checker refuses a write
※ from anything but a constructor's own body (4373), which is what makes a
※ program never stop on the fault the pages would give it.
※
※ **What is in it may be anything the variable holds in its own bytes**: a
※ number, an array, a record, a reference into the image -- which the image
※ is written with, the address being known before the program runs.  What
※ changes without the variable being written is refused (4372).
※
※ Compare: **C** with GCC or Clang, where `__attribute__((section(...)))` and a
※ call to `mprotect` from the last constructor is the way to have this and
※ nothing checks the rest of the program keeps to it; the linker's RELRO, which
※ does the same for what the dynamic loader writes, and only that; **Rust**,
※ whose `std::sync::OnceLock` is written once at run time and checked on every
※ read rather than protected; **Go**, whose package `init` functions write
※ variables nothing then stops anyone writing; **D**, whose `immutable` module
※ variables set in a `shared static this()` constructor are this rule, checked
※ the same way and not protected.

let std := ⎕import("std")

type Found = start : u64 ; count : u32

let fallback: u64 = 5u64

@[relro]
let seed: mut u64 = 1u64

@[relro]
let squares: mut u32⟦8⟧ = ⟦0u32, 0u32, 0u32, 0u32, 0u32, 0u32, 0u32, 0u32⟧

@[relro]
let found: mut Found = Found(.start ← 0u64, .count ← 0u32)

@[relro]
let chosen: mut &static u64 = &fallback

@[constructor, impure]
fn prepare():
    seed ← 7u64
    let n: mut u32 = 0u32
    foreach &mut square := squares:
        square⌖ ← n × n
        n ← n + 1u32
    found.start ← 4096u64
    found.count ← 3u32

@[constructor(priority=1), impure]
fn later():
    ※ A constructor that runs after another reads what that one wrote, and
    ※ may write on top of it.
    seed ← seed × 6u64

@[startup]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    if seed ≠ 42u64:
        1u6
    elif squares⟦7⟧ ≠ 49u32 ∨ squares⟦3⟧ ≠ 9u32:
        2u6
    elif found.start ≠ 4096u64 ∨ found.count ≠ 3u32:
        3u6
    elif chosen⌖ ≠ 5u64:
        4u6
    else:
        0u6
